Hello Bob

This is not a multinomial model, sorry I confused..I will include covariates for this model later..

This is a (multiple outcomes) binomial model.

I just want to calculate beta0 (logit(p)) for 4 outcomes (multivariate)  for each Id (CCG)  by drawing the normal random effects from a multivariate normal.

my univariate (single outcome) model is working and I want to do the same for multivariate model.

thanks

On Thursday, May 8, 2014 12:54:17 AM UTC+1, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
> On May 8, 2014, at 1:32 AM, vera <kandasa...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> > Hello Bob
> >
> > Thanks for your comments.
> >
> >  "log logit likelihood" ???
> >
> > log[ Binomial(n/N, theta) ]=const + nlog(theta) + (N-n)log(1-theta)
>
> I see --- that's just the log of the binomial pmf.
> I tend to think of "log likelihood" as the piece of a model
> that gives log p(data|parameters,constants).
>
> >
> > (it is on page 308 in manual)
> >
> > logit(theta)=beta0 (lod odds)
> >
> > replacing theta by beta0  gives the following
> >
> > log[ Binomial(n/N, beta0) ] = nbeta0 - N log(1+exp(beta0))
> >
> > ( call this  "log logit likelihood" )
> >
> > Yes, p is p-values (theta).
> >
> > The question is how to use increment_log_prob for :
> >
> > log[ Binomial(n/N, beta0) ] = nbeta0 - N log(1+exp(beta0)) to find beta0 values in stan...
> >
> > obviously  Numer[j,i] ~ binomial_logit(Deno[j,i], beta0[ID[j],i]); won't work as you mentioned there is no
> >
> >  multinomial logit coded directly in stan.
>
> Do you mean binomial here?  For multinomial equivalent of logit,
> you just want to use softmax explicitly.
>
> >
> >  I tried:
> >
> >  increment_log_prob( Numer[j,i]*beta0[CCG[j],i]-Deno[j,i]*log1p_exp(exp(beta0[CCG[j],i])));
> >
> >   increment_log_prob( Numer[j,i]*beta0[CCG[j],i]-Deno[j,i]*log(1+exp(beta0[CCG[j],i])));
> >
> > both giving the same ouputs but not sensible outputs...
> >
> > I know I am not using  increment_log_prob for my  "log logit likelihood" correctly
>
> I still don't quite understand what your intent is from the formulas
> above.  They're not multinomial.
>
> You can just use inv_logit(alpha) on the log odds alpha and then plug
> that into the binomial if you want, but I'm somehow thinking that's
> not what you're asking.
>
> The other problem I see is this:
>
> > transformed parameters {
> > >
> > >  real<lower=0,upper=1> p[M,noc];
> > >
> > >  for(i in 1:4)
> > >  {
> > >  for (j in 1:M)
> > >  {
> > >     p[j,i] <- inv_logit(beta0[j,i]);
> > > }
> > > }
> > > }
>
> You can't just apply inv_logit to the individual arguments. I'm guessing
> what you want here is (a) a simplex data type --- that's for vectors
> that sum to 1, and then softmax to convert the logit-scaled probabilities
> to a simplex.
>
> - Bob
>
> >
> > thanks for your help..
> >
> >
> > some outputs:
> >
> > p[33,1]      0.9987
> > p[33,2]      0.9997
> > p[33,3]      0.9990
> > p[33,4]      1.0034
> > p[34,1]      0.9988
> > p[34,2]      0.9994
> >
> >
> >
> > On Wednesday, May 7, 2014 12:31:50 AM UTC+1, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > You mean values of the parameter "p" or do you mean "p values",
> > the stats concept?
> >
> > I'm also not sure what you mean by "log logit likelihood".
> > I see that there's a comment with that content in your file.
> > All you need to do is give Stan the log-odds as a parameter
> > to binomial_logit, because
> >
> >   binomial_logit(N, alpha) =def= binomial(N, inv_logit(alpha))
> >
> > Stan doesn't (at least yet) discriminate between the prior
> > and the likelihood --- they're all just part of the model.
> >
> > As to suggestions for your model, you need to be careful with
> > arithmetic depending on where your parameters go.  Stan has
> > more stable built-in functions for things like log(1 + x) [log1p]
> > and for addition on the log scale (log_sum_exp).  You probably
> > don't want to use what you have:
> >
> > > increment_log_prob( Numer[j,i]*log(exp(beta0[ID[j],i]))-Deno[j,i]*log(1+exp(beta0[CCG[j],i])));
> >
> >
> > First of all, log(exp(a)) = a, whereas exp(a) is prone to overflow.
> > We also have built in:
> >
> >   log1p_exp(a) =def= log(1 + exp(a))
> >
> > These are common operations.
> >
> > We are also not recommending inverse Wishart priors --- see the
> > manual for the scaled correlation matrices we do recommend --- but
> > this is a modeling choice.  Stan doesn't need to rely on conjugacy
> > for the multivariate distributions.
> >
> > - Bob
