Vera,

I ran your model with rstan Version 2.2.0 and I couldn't reproduce the error.

I used range() on the output list to see if any draw of p was above 1 but that was not the case.  I repeated the operation just in case but the output were pretty similar.

The values of  p1 ranged between (0.641 - 0.824), p2 ranged between (0.734 - 0.993),  p3 ranged between (0.559 - 0.943) and p4 ranged between (0.737 - 0.962).

Thew only thing I can see above 1 for p1, p2, p3 and p4 are the Rhat values if you use the print function.

Looking more closely at the output printed in the file models.pdf I get the feeling that you are looking at this column when you report values above 1. The Rhat values I get from the model happen to follow a distribution that is pretty similar to the numbers in the document.

The Rhat values are not bounded between 0 and 1. They can take pretty much any value but you want them to below 1.1 or 1.2. A good look at the manual should clear that up.

Good luck with your modelling

Chris
