Hi,

I try to understand differences between log likelihood calculated by

x  ~ normal(m, s);

and

lp <- normal_log(x,m,s)

My expectation was that the two should differ only by a constant. This is not what I get (below). What do I missunderstand?

 <https://lh6.googleusercontent.com/-EihCMAfL22I/U3Y6Ju95s_I/AAAAAAAAFf0/kjbmiBA2BYw/s1600/test.png>

Model is

data {

  int<lower=0> N;

  vector[N] x;

}

parameters {

  real m;

  real<lower=0> s;

}

model {

    x  ~ normal(m, s);  // fit

}

generated quantities {

    real lp;

    lp <- normal_log(x,m,s);

}

code and documentation is attached.
Best regards,

Christian

From u2a7f346506a3e9ff at groups.google.com  Fri May 16 16:32:24 2014
From: u2a7f346506a3e9ff at groups.google.com (Marcus Brubaker)
Date: Fri, 16 May 2014 16:32:24 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] difference: x ~ normal(m, s) versus lp <- normal_log(x,m,s)
Message-ID: <O7YAveO_qiI.REJETvsYs2wJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <O7YAveO_qiI.4KEooMieXlgJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <O7YAveO_qiI.4KEooMieXlgJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/O7YAveO_qiI/m/REJETvsYs2wJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

There is an additional term that is needed because Stan (under the hood) does a non-linear reparameterization of s in order to enforce the lower bound.  To correct for that, the log determinant of the transformation Jacobian is added to lp__.  See chapter 17 of the Stan manual for more on this.

Cheers,
Marcus

