These problems are all the same.  You're passing in functions
of the wrong type.

The problem is I don't know what you're intent is in trying to
do something undefined.  The latest case is:

-----------------------------------------------

mu <- tail( phi , T-1 ) .* tail( beta , T-1 ) ; // elementwise multiplication
                                              ^-- here

DIAGNOSTIC(S) FROM PARSER:
no matches for function name="elt_multiply"
    arg 0 type=vector[1]
    arg 1 type=vector[1]
available function signatures for elt_multiply:
0.  elt_multiply(vector, vector) : vector
1.  elt_multiply(row vector, row vector) : row vector
2.  elt_multiply(matrix, matrix) : matrix

------------------------------------------------------

Here you try to use two arrays of vectors as arguments ---
that's what the vector[1] means (we have an outstanding issue
to clean up this notation because it's confusing users).

But the elt_multiply function only accepts two vectors as arguments.

- Bob

