On May 30, 2014, at 10:56 PM, Ross Boylan <ro...@biostat.ucsf.edu> wrote:

> On 5/30/2014 11:28 AM, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>> On May 30, 2014, at 8:03 PM, Ross Boylan <ro...@biostat.ucsf.edu> wrote:
>>
>>> I have estimates of the distribution of an effect across K groups, where K is small, like 3.  The overall goal is to get an estimate of the the variability of the effect across groups.  For each set of coefficients, call it B_j for the j'th set, g_kj is the group effect for the k'th group.
>>>
>>> One can compute the variance of g across k for each j, and have a posterior distribution for the variance.
>>>
>>> Should the individual variances use K or K-1 as the divisor?
>>> If B_j actually were the correct coefficients it seems to me K would be more appropriate--although I suppose even in that case we are still estimating g_kj since we only have a sample of the target population k.
>> The point would be to assume the K you observe are part of
>> some larger population you're trying to estimate.  In
>> that case, 1/K yields the maximum likelihood estimator and 1/(K-1)
>> an unbiased estimator.
> The categories here are ethnic groups.  Taking them (the categories, not the individuals in them) as part of a larger population of categories seems unnatural.  The division into 3 includes most groups (whites being the notable exception), and there is another division into 5 racial categories that completely partitions the space ("other" is one of the 5 categories).  The 3 categories are a subset of the 5 categories, and so are part of a slightly larger (but far from infinite) population.  But the 5 aren't part of anything larger.
>

That's easy, then --- 1/K is what to use for the population variance.

> The racial/ethnic groups are themselves social constructs, and the degree of refinement---saying that there is a group that is Latino as opposed to separate groups for each country or separate groups for Latino based on skin color---it itself an analytic decision rather than a feature of nature.
>>
>> I would think the variance across groups would be very sensitive
>> to the priors you're using unless there's a lot of data per group.
> If you mean observed data, we have several hundred per ethnic group.

Yes, that should be enough that the priors shouldn't have too strong
an effect.  Never a bad idea to test their sensitivity, though.

>>> I'm also planning on using the range of the g_kj across k as a measure of dispersion.
>> Tail measures like 95% intervals are hard to estimate and require many more
>> samples.
> The range doesn't seem much like a tail measure, at least if K is low.  The g are already averages, and I would think the distribution of differences between them has more to do with the fat part of the distributions.  We do have some situation with 15 (3x5) categories, and the maximum difference statistic seems more problematic there.
>

I see.  That makes sense.  You're not looking at extreme
values for a single parameter, but comparing parameters.
If you compute these in Stan (use the generated quantities block),
you can measure convergence and effective sample sizes.

> Concretely, suppose we knew that African-American (AA) respondents with Asian/Pacific Islander (API) partners had a 50% chance of having risky sex and all 14 other combinations had a 30% chance of risky sex.  The variance of the respondent x partner interaction is modest in this case, and if we did a frequentist test for the equality of all effects it would likely come out with "data consistent with the null" for samples that weren't too big.  Despite low variance and unimpressive p, I would consider the interaction important in this case.  That's the kind of thing I'm trying to capture with the range statistic.
>>
